  "biobricks:reviewed": false,
  "biobricks:reviewNotes": "Determined by the BioBricks licensing-provenance annotation agent (slice items[1332:1338]). PubChem has no single license: NCBI states US-Government-created content is public domain, but PubChem specifically aggregates depositor-contributed material that may be copyright-protected, and NCBI explicitly warns that anyone making commercial use must adhere to each copyright holder's terms and that NCBI cannot grant such permission. Because this brick bundles the whole FTP dump (compounds + bioassays + annotations) across many depositors, the aggregate license cannot be pinned to one class and commercial redistribution rights vary per record; classed conservatively as unknown / commercial unknown (most-restrictive-component rule). HUMAN REVIEW: PubChem's bulk compound/bioassay records are widely treated as freely usable, but depositor-restricted subsets (and any DrugBank/HSDB-style annotations) must be excluded or separately licensed before commercial redistribution.",
